.\" tcptarpit(8) - manual page in mdoc(7), formatted for OpenBSD.
.\"
.Dd October 31, 2026
.Dt TCPTARPIT 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm tcptarpit
.Nd generic layer-4 TCP tarpit for abusive clients
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Ar config-file
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
daemon traps abusive TCP clients in connections that never answer.
It listens on a loopback address and port (127.0.0.1:8024 by default),
accepts every connection diverted to it by
.Xr pf 4 ,
and then never sends a single byte back.
Incoming data is read and discarded, so the client's TCP window runs
full while it waits for a response that never comes.
.Pp
Sources reach the tarpit through the same mechanism as
.Xr spamd 8 :
.Xr pf.conf 5
rules using
.Ic max-src-conn-rate
with
.Ic overload
move offending addresses into the
.Ao bruteforcers Ac
pf table, and an
.Ic rdr-to
rule diverts subsequent connections from listed addresses to the
daemon.
The daemon is protocol agnostic and traps SSH brute forcers as well as
HTTP scanners and directory busters.
.Pp
Connections are held until the client gives up, or until they exceed
the configured maximum age, at which point they are closed with a
normal FIN during the next cleanup pass.
.Pp
The daemon is started via
.Xr rc.d 8
and
.Xr rcctl 8 ;
see
.Sx EXAMPLES .
Output is sent to
.Xr syslogd 8
at
.Dq daemon.info
through
.Xr daemon 8 .
.Sh CONFIGURATION
If present,
.Nm
reads its configuration from
.Pa /etc/tcptarpit.conf .
If the file is missing, built-in defaults are used and a warning is
logged.
A malformed file aborts startup with a
.Pa file : line
error message.
An alternative configuration file can be given as the only argument.
.Pp
The following keys are recognized:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic listen_ip
Address to listen on.
Default:
.Li 127.0.0.1 .
.It Ic listen_port
Port to listen on; must match the
.Ic rdr-to
rule in
.Xr pf.conf 5 .
Default:
.Li 8024 .
.It Ic cleanup_interval
Seconds between cleanup passes over stale connections.
Default:
.Li 1800 .
.It Ic max_age
Maximum age of a held connection in seconds before it is closed.
The effective hold time lies between
.Ic max_age
and
.Ic max_age
plus
.Ic cleanup_interval .
Default:
.Li 1800 .
.It Ic log_file
Log into this file instead of stdout.
Empty (the default) logs to stdout, which
.Xr daemon 8
pipes to
.Xr syslogd 8
at
.Dq daemon.info .
.It Ic pid_file
Write the process id to this file on startup and remove it on exit.
Used by
.Xr newsyslog 8
to signal the daemon after log rotation.
.It Ic user
Drop privileges to this user after the listen socket, log file and pid
file have been opened.
Empty (the default) performs no drop; if the daemon already runs as
that user, the drop is a no-op.
.It Ic group
Drop to this group; empty (the default) uses the primary group of
.Ic user .
.El
.Pp
On
.Dv SIGHUP
the daemon reopens its log file, so
.Xr newsyslog 8
rotation works with a
.Ic log_file
and
.Ic pid_file
combination.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Install the daemon, enable and start it at boot:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
install -m 0755 tcptarpit.pl /usr/local/bin/tcptarpit.pl
install -m 0555 tcptarpit /etc/rc.d/tcptarpit
install -m 0644 tcptarpit.conf.sample /etc/tcptarpit.conf
install -m 0444 tcptarpit.8 /usr/local/man/man8/tcptarpit.8

rcctl enable tcptarpit
rcctl start tcptarpit
.Ed
.Pp
Typical
.Xr pf.conf 5
ruleset; the tarpit redirect must precede the ordinary pass rules:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
ext_if = "em0"

table <bruteforcers> persist

# never tarpit the admins
table <admins> const { 198.51.100.10 }
pass in quick on $ext_if from <admins>

# everyone on the list goes straight into the tarpit
pass in quick on $ext_if proto tcp from <bruteforcers> \e
    to any port { ssh, http, https } rdr-to 127.0.0.1 port 8024

# SSH watch: more than 3 connections within 30 seconds goes on the list
pass in on $ext_if proto tcp to any port ssh flags S/SA keep state \e
    (max-src-conn-rate 3/30, overload <bruteforcers> flush global)

# webserver watch
pass in on $ext_if proto tcp to any port { http, https } \e
    flags S/SA keep state \e
    (max-src-conn-rate 30/10, overload <bruteforcers> flush global)
.Ed
.Pp
Inspect and edit the address list with
.Xr pfctl 8 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
pfctl -t bruteforcers -T show
pfctl -t bruteforcers -T delete 203.0.113.7
.Ed
.Pp
Install from the package (recommended): the
.Xr pkg_add 1
step creates the unprivileged
.Dq _tcptarpit
user and group automatically
.Pq Pa @newuser Ns / Ns Pa @newgroup No in the package PLIST,
and the rc script runs the daemon as that user.
.Pp
Run unprivileged with a dedicated log file and rotation (manual
installation):
.Bd -literal -offset indent
groupadd -g 960 _tcptarpit
useradd -g _tcptarpit -c "tcptarpit daemon" -d /nonexistent \e
    -s /sbin/nologin _tcptarpit
mkdir -p /var/run/tcptarpit && chown _tcptarpit /var/run/tcptarpit
install -o _tcptarpit -g _tcptarpit -m 0640 /dev/null /var/log/tcptarpit.log
.Ed
.Pp
In
.Pa /etc/tcptarpit.conf :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
user=_tcptarpit
log_file=/var/log/tcptarpit.log
pid_file=/var/run/tcptarpit/tcptarpit.pid
.Ed
.Pp
In
.Pa /etc/newsyslog.conf :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
/var/log/tcptarpit.log _tcptarpit:_tcptarpit 640 7 250 * Z /var/run/tcptarpit/tcptarpit.pid
.Ed
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /etc/tcptarpit.conf
Daemon configuration.
.It Pa /usr/local/bin/tcptarpit.pl
The daemon itself.
.It Pa /etc/rc.d/tcptarpit
.Xr rc.d 8
control script.
.It Pa /var/log/tcptarpit.log
Optional log file (see
.Ic log_file ) .
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr pf 4 ,
.Xr pf.conf 5 ,
.Xr daemon 8 ,
.Xr pfctl 8 ,
.Xr rc 8 ,
.Xr rc.d 8 ,
.Xr rc.conf 8 ,
.Xr rcctl 8 ,
.Xr spamd 8 ,
.Xr syslogd 8
.Sh CAVEATS
Only IPv4 is supported.
The number of simultaneously held connections is bounded by the process
file descriptor limit.
Because listing is based on connection rates, legitimate clients that
exceed the same rate can end up in the tarpit as well; keep trusted
addresses in an exemption table that is matched before the redirect.
.Xr pf 4
table entries are kept by the running kernel only and do not survive a
reboot unless the table is backed by a file.
